{"@context":["https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/ns/content/v1.jsonld",{"@base":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio/"}],"@id":"papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/prop-vdw-pskew-isolation","@type":["folio-assistant-core:Proposition","doco:Section"],"label":"prop:vdw-pskew-isolation","kind":"proposition","title":"Van der Waals as the bar-odd ($P_{\\mathrm{skew}}$, sinh) channel","uses":["papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/prop-hopf-link-symmetric-decomposition","papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/def-van-der-waals-hopf-channel","papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/prop-crossing-energy-decomposition","papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/def-bar-involution","papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/rem-im-q-vs-p-skew","papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/prop-alpha-em-habiro-bar-involution","papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/thm-em-spectral-completeness","papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/cor-gauge-coupling-uniqueness","papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/prop-alpha-em-categorical-trace-ratio"],"tags":["van-der-waals","bar-involution","p-skew","sinh-cosh","hopf-link","rapidity","narrowed-probe"],"leanRef":"qou:QOU.VdwPskew.hopf_bar_even_odd_decomposition","text":"vdw-pskew-isolation.md","leanSource":"folio/quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/vdw-pskew-isolation.lean","provenance":"authored","html":"<a id=\"prop:vdw-pskew-isolation\"></a><p><em>Under the bar involution <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q \\mapsto q^{-1}</code>, the Hopf weighting identically splits into a bar-even and a bar-odd part. With rapidity <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\lambda = \\ln q</code>:</em></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-math math-display\">  2\\,\\sigma_1^2 = \\underbrace{2\\cosh(2\\lambda)\\,\\mathbf 1}_{\\text{bar-even (retained)}} + \\underbrace{2\\sinh(2\\lambda)\\,(e_+ - e_-)}_{\\text{bar-odd } (P_{\\mathrm{skew}})}.</code></pre>\n<p><em>This explicitly uses the formal identities <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q^2+q^{-2}=2\\cosh 2\\lambda</code>, <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q^2-q^{-2}=2\\sinh 2\\lambda</code>, and <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">e_+ + e_- = \\mathbf 1</code>.</em></p>\n","source":"folio/quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/vdw-pskew-isolation.md","leanStatus":"proved","heading":"Proposition","number":"21.10.8"}
